$DIR/main.p:5:15: string literal "\x7fELF\xff" is not valid UTF-8; use a []byte for binary data
    Every string in Plutonium holds valid UTF-8 text, so a string literal whose
    escapes spell out bytes that are not valid UTF-8 is an error. Here the
    escapes make a byte or a sequence of bytes that does not encode a character.
    Binary data belongs in a []byte or a byte array, which may hold any bytes.
    To write the character itself, use its \u escape or type it.
    Go strings may hold any bytes. Plutonium guarantees that a string is text,
    so that ranging over it, slicing it between runes and the library's rune
    functions never meet broken encodings, and so that ASCII strings can be
    recognized once and taken fast paths for.
    Keep binary data in bytes:
        magic := []byte{0x7f, 'E', 'L', 'F'}
    (see "String literals" in the Plutonium spec, section 4.11)
    (run "pluto explain E2012" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:9:21: string literal "\xed\xa0\x80" is not valid UTF-8; use a []byte for binary data
    (explained above; run "pluto explain E2012" for more)
